+\subsection{Spatial Supervision on Actual Dataset Volumes}
+\label{sec:dataset-constraint-examples}
+
+Figures~\ref{fig:msd-constraint-example}--\ref{fig:geofwi3d-constraint-example}
+use the same cases as the dataset illustrations: \texttt{hippocampus\_001},
+\texttt{hippocampus\_adni\_10007\_L} and \texttt{model\_0001}.
+Their native grids contain $35\times51\times35$, $32\times48\times40$
+and $96\times96\times96$ voxels, respectively. All morphology, distance
+fields, depth layers and counts are computed in three dimensions before
+rendering. The examples illustrate the definitions on unaugmented native
+grids; training applies the constraints to the grid specified by each
+recipe, which can change the available surrounding region and its weights.
+
+Panel (a) shows the intact three-dimensional annotation. Panels (b) and (c)
+show two-step, six-neighbour bands and the in-plane subset of their selected
+voxel pairs. Bands and edges use the whole foreground, including the union
+of anterior and posterior hippocampus in MSD. Blue pair segments connect
+equal foreground-membership labels; orange segments cross between foreground
+and background. The anatomical division within MSD is therefore absent from
+this pair target. The geological example retains zero-padded morphology at
+the cube faces, as in three of the four conventional model recipes. UNETR's
+frame exclusion is specified in Appendix~\ref{sec:geo-boundaries}.
+
+The band view is the source-axis-2 slice containing the most foreground
+voxels, with the lowest index breaking ties. The yellow square identifies
+the $16\times16$ area enlarged in the pair view. Its centre is placed near
+the in-plane foreground boundary closest to the slice's foreground centroid,
+away from the crop faces where possible. Pairs extending out of the displayed
+plane still enter the three-dimensional objective but are not drawn.
+
+Panel (d) renders the signed-distance field of anterior hippocampus for MSD
+and of the single foreground class for ADNI and GeoFWI3D. Distances use the
+half-voxel convention, clipping at eight voxels and division by eight. The
+rendering retains the class and exterior field values up to four voxel units;
+a quadrant is cut away to expose the interior. These display choices do not
+alter the computed field. The intact class mask in (a) provides its context.
+
+Panels (e) and (f) explain depth balancing. The bars in (e) show each layer's
+share of the surrounding region's voxels; the line shows its share of the
+loss weight. The latter is equal across layers with at least 64 voxels and
+smaller for sparser layers. Panel (f) shows five occupied layers sampled
+uniformly across the ordered layer indices, including both extremes. Each
+retains its full three-dimensional shape and is translated along the plane
+normal to separate it from its neighbours. Colours use the voxel weights
+computed from \emph{all} layers, divided by their mean over the full region;
+they do not depict prediction errors. The common colour scale is logarithmic.
+
+MSD uses its verified physical coordinate map, with two-millimetre layers
+and an eight-millimetre surrounding margin. ADNI and GeoFWI3D use two-voxel
+layers and an eight-voxel margin in source-array coordinates. The reference
+plane follows the dataset-specific construction described in
+Chapter~\ref{sec:methodology}. None of these examples requires the geological
+fallback for a nearly parallel principal axis. Volumes are rotated for
+presentation without smoothing or resampling their voxel faces; rendered
+sizes are fitted independently and do not imply a common physical scale.
+
